{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9Av8mhiwP3HZ5VGfEE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014zF7gZ5XWW2k9B8qJ4joXb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211944__x51597c/work/.research/engine/unpacked/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2240,"limit":590},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27079,"cache_read_input_tokens":114192,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27079},"output_tokens":75,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9dbc4055-18d5-4f5b-aa46-86fd0623cd25","uuid":"b67b4a57-0765-46b7-bf61-02d8ffa79293","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:22:02.626Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9Austm3yRk6NtSE1TU","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014zF7gZ5XWW2k9B8qJ4joXb":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211944__x51597c/work/.research/engine/unpacked/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2240,"limit":590}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014zF7gZ5XWW2k9B8qJ4joXb","type":"tool_result","content":"2240\t## 7. Input and touch\n2241\t\n2242\t### applyDeadzone()\n2243\t\n2244\t```ts\n2245\tfunction applyDeadzone(x: number, y: number, deadzone = 0.2): { x: number; y: number }\n2246\t```\n2247\t\n2248\tRadial deadzone with rescaling: inside `deadzone` reads 0, the rim reads 1, and the\n2249\tdirection is kept (no square-gate snapping to the axes).\n2250\t\n2251\t### interface ButtonEvent\n2252\t\n2253\tA pointer button pressed or let go (`Input.buttonEvents`): its key, and where the pointer was (normalised device coordinates).\n2254\t\n2255\t- `readonly code: string`\n2256\t- `readonly down: boolean`\n2257\t- `readonly x: number`\n2258\t- `readonly y: number`\n2259\t\n2260\t### DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS\n2261\t\n2262\t```ts\n2263\tconst DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS: readonly TouchButton[]\n2264\t```\n2265\t\n2266\tDefault buttons for PlatformerCharacter (see character/controls.ts KEYMAP).\n2267\t\n2268\t### GAMEPAD_BUTTONS\n2269\t\n2270\t```ts\n2271\tconst GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>\n2272\t```\n2273\t\n2274\tStandard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\n2275\tKEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \"standard\" layout:\n2276\t0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\n2277\t\n2278\t### interface GamepadLike\n2279\t\n2280\tThe parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects).\n2281\t\n2282\t- `readonly connected?: boolean`\n2283\t- `readonly mapping?: string`\n2284\t- `readonly axes: readonly number[]`\n2285\t- `readonly buttons: readonly { readonly pressed: boolean; readonly value?: number }[]`\n2286\t\n2287\t### class Input\n2288\t\n2289\tKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n2290\t- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n2291\t  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n2292\t- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n2293\t  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n2294\t  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n2295\t- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n2296\t  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n2297\t  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n2298\t  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n2299\t  a frame is not lost.\n2300\t- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n2301\t\n2302\t- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n2303\t- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n2304\t- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n2305\t- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n2306\t- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n2307\t- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n2308\t- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n2309\t- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n2310\t- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n2311\t- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n2312\t- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n2313\t- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n2314\t- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n2315\t- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n2316\t- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n2317\t- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n2318\t- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n2319\t- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n2320\t- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n2321\t- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n2322\t- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n2323\t- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n2324\t- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n2325\t- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n2326\t- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n2327\t- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n2328\t- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n2329\t- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n2330\t- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n2331\t- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n2332\t- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n2333\t- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n2334\t- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n2335\t- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n2336\t- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n2337\t- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n2338\t- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n2339\t- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n2340\t- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n2341\t- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n2342\t- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n2343\t- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n2344\t- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n2345\t- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n2346\t\n2347\t### interface InputFrame\n2348\t\n2349\tWhat the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\n2350\tOptional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\n2351\tfrom the frame before.\n2352\t\n2353\t- `dt: number`: Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it).\n2354\t- `held?: string[]`: Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted.\n2355\t- `pressed?: string[]`: Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held).\n2356\t- `analog?: [number, number]`: The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone).\n2357\t- `pad?: [number, number]`\n2358\t- `delta?: [number, number]`: Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame.\n2359\t- `wheel?: number`\n2360\t- `pointer?: [number, number, number, number]`: Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says).\n2361\t- `gamepad?: boolean`: Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes.\n2362\t- `touch?: boolean`: Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes.\n2363\t- `clicks?: [string, 0 | 1, number, number][]`: Pointer buttons pressed (1) and let go (0) this frame, in order, each where the pointer was (`buttonEvents`).\n2364\t- `commands?: unknown[]`: Data from outside the canvas delivered this frame (`send()`): a DOM widget's value, a save read from storage.\n2365\t- `sync?: true`: Ran in the same task as the frame before (frames of one `step(n)` call): no promise callbacks ran between them. A replay waits for them before every other frame.\n2366\t\n2367\t### interface InputState\n2368\t\n2369\tWhat input a later frame still reads, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): keys held, presses not yet used and how old they are, the pointer.\n2370\t\n2371\t- `readonly held: readonly string[]`\n2372\t- `readonly queued: readonly (readonly [string, number])[]`: Queued presses: [key, seconds of game time since it was pressed].\n2373\t- `readonly pointer: readonly [number, number, number, number]`\n2374\t- `readonly gamepad: boolean`\n2375\t- `readonly outbox: readonly unknown[]`: Data sent (`send`) and not yet delivered: the next frame that runs delivers it.\n2376\t\n2377\t### MOUSE_BUTTONS\n2378\t\n2379\t```ts\n2380\tconst MOUSE_BUTTONS: readonly (readonly [code: string, bit: number])[]\n2381\t```\n2382\t\n2383\tMouse buttons as key codes (`isDown('MouseLeft')`, `consumePress('MouseRight')`), each with\n2384\tits `PointerEvent.buttons` bit, by `PointerEvent.button` (0 left, 1 middle, 2 right).\n2385\t\n2386\t### PRESS_WINDOW\n2387\t\n2388\t```ts\n2389\tconst PRESS_WINDOW: 0.15\n2390\t```\n2391\t\n2392\tHow long a queued press stays consumable, in seconds of game time.\n2393\t\n2394\t### TOUCH_KEY\n2395\t\n2396\t```ts\n2397\tconst TOUCH_KEY: \"Touch\"\n2398\t```\n2399\t\n2400\tA finger on the canvas, as a key: held while one is down (not a mouse button, so a tap is not a click).\n2401\t\n2402\t### interface TouchButton\n2403\t\n2404\t- `label: string`\n2405\t- `code: string`: Key code this button presses (e.g. 'Space').\n2406\t- `hint?: string`: Short hint shown under the label.\n2407\t- `at?: { x: number; y: number; size?: number; corner?: TouchCorner }`: Place the button yourself instead of in the default grid: its centre, in CSS pixels from `corner` (default bottom-right; `x` grows inward from the side, `y` inward from the top or bottom edge), and its diameter (`size`, default 58). An action game's cluster (…\n2408\t\n2409\t### interface TouchButtonState\n2410\t\n2411\tWhat a button shows right now (`TouchControls.set`): a game drives its buttons from its own\n2412\tstate each frame (skill icons, cooldowns, costs, a contextual action). Only what changed\n2413\ttouches the DOM.\n2414\t\n2415\t- `label?: string`\n2416\t- `hint?: string`\n2417\t- `icon?: HTMLCanvasElement | HTMLImageElement | null`: A picture instead of the label (a canvas or image; drawn pixelated); null removes it.\n2418\t- `cooldown?: number`: Cooldown shutter, 0..1 of the button covered (0 = ready).\n2419\t- `timer?: string`: Text over the shutter (seconds left).\n2420\t- `badge?: string`: A small corner badge (a cost, a count).\n2421\t- `disabled?: boolean`: Dimmed (can't be used now); it still presses its key.\n2422\t- `hidden?: boolean`: Not shown (its place is kept), and its key is released.\n2423\t\n2424\t### class TouchControls\n2425\t\n2426\tOn-screen controls for phones and tablets: a virtual joystick (bottom-left) that feeds\n2427\t`input.analog`, action buttons (bottom-right, in a grid or placed one by one with `at`)\n2428\tthat press key codes, and a small top bar (debug panel, Pixel/Raw, resolution, looks).\n2429\tCamera: drag on the game to orbit/look, pinch to zoom (handled by Input.attachPointer).\n2430\tShown automatically on coarse pointers, or with `?touch=1`.\n2431\t\n2432\tA game drives it at runtime: `set(code, state)` (icon, cooldown, badge, disabled, hidden),\n2433\t`show(false)` while a menu owns the screen, and `rects()` to lay its HUD out around the\n2434\tcontrols.\n2435\t\n2436\t- `new TouchControls(container: HTMLElement, input: Input, buttons: readonly TouchButton[] = DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS, topBar: readonly TouchButton[] = [])`\n2437\t- `static wanted(search = location.search): boolean`\n2438\t- `readonly root: HTMLDivElement`\n2439\t- `set(code: string, state: TouchButtonState): void`: Update every button that presses `code` (see TouchButtonState).\n2440\t- `show(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the joystick and the action buttons (the top bar stays): hide them while a menu or a full-screen view owns the screen. Hiding releases every held key and the stick.\n2441\t- `get visible: boolean`\n2442\t- `rects(): { stick: DOMRect | null; buttons: { code: string; label: string; hidden: boolean; rect: DOMRect }[]; bar: DOMRect | null }`: Client rects (CSS pixels) of the controls on screen now: the joystick and every button, with the key code it presses. A hidden button keeps its place (`hidden` says it isn't drawn), so a HUD laid out around them doesn't move when a contextual button comes and…\n2443\t\n2444\t### type TouchCorner\n2445\t\n2446\t```ts\n2447\ttype TouchCorner = 'bottom-right' | 'bottom-left' | 'top-right' | 'top-left'\n2448\t```\n2449\t\n2450\tCorner of the screen a placed touch button is measured from (inside the safe area).\n2451\t\n2452\t## 8. Audio\n2453\t\n2454\t### attenuation()\n2455\t\n2456\t```ts\n2457\tfunction attenuation(distance: number, o: SpatialOptions = {}): number\n2458\t```\n2459\t\n2460\tVolume at `distance` (m): 1 inside `ref`, then inverse distance, fading to 0 at `max`.\n2461\t\n2462\t### class AudioManager\n2463\t\n2464\tWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\n2465\tsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n2466\tpersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n2467\t\n2468\tBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\n2469\tfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n2470\t(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\n2471\twas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n2472\t\n2473\t- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n2474\t- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n2475\t- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n2476\t- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n2477\t- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n2478\t- `context: AudioContext | null`\n2479\t- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n2480\t- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n2481\t- `get muted: boolean`\n2482\t- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n2483\t- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n2484\t- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n2485\t- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n2486\t- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n2487\t- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n2488\t- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n2489\t- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n2490\t- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n2491\t- `stopMusic(): void`\n2492\t- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n2493\t- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n2494\t- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n2495\t- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n2496\t- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n2497\t- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n2498\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n2499\t\n2500\t### interface AudioSettings\n2501\t\n2502\t- `master: number`\n2503\t- `sfx: number`\n2504\t- `music: number`\n2505\t- `muted: boolean`\n2506\t\n2507\t### type Instrument\n2508\t\n2509\t```ts\n2510\ttype Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>\n2511\t```\n2512\t\n2513\tA track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those).\n2514\t\n2515\t### interface Listener\n2516\t\n2517\t- `position: V3Like`\n2518\t- `right: V3Like`: The listener's right, in world space (unit): the camera's right for a third-person game.\n2519\t\n2520\t### muffleCutoff\n2521\t\n2522\t```ts\n2523\tconst muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number\n2524\t```\n2525\t\n2526\tLow-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud.\n2527\t\n2528\t### noteFrequency()\n2529\t\n2530\t```ts\n2531\tfunction noteFrequency(note: string): number | null\n2532\t```\n2533\t\n2534\t'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note.\n2535\t\n2536\t### parseSong()\n2537\t\n2538\t```ts\n2539\tfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n2540\t```\n2541\t\n2542\tFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n2543\t\n2544\t### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n2545\t\n2546\t```ts\n2547\tconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n2548\t```\n2549\t\n2550\tThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n2551\t\n2552\t### interface PlayOptions\n2553\t\n2554\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n2555\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n2556\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n2557\t\n2558\t### renderSound()\n2559\t\n2560\t```ts\n2561\tfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n2562\t```\n2563\t\n2564\tRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n2565\t\n2566\t### SFX\n2567\t\n2568\t```ts\n2569\tconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n2570\t```\n2571\t\n2572\tBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n2573\t`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\n2574\tSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n2575\t\n2576\t### type SfxName\n2577\t\n2578\t```ts\n2579\ttype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n2580\t```\n2581\t\n2582\t### interface Song\n2583\t\n2584\tA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\n2585\twhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\n2586\tEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\n2587\tnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n2588\t\n2589\t- `bpm: number`\n2590\t- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n2591\t- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n2592\t- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n2593\t- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n2594\t- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n2595\t\n2596\t### interface SoundDef\n2597\t\n2598\t- `wave: Wave`\n2599\t- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n2600\t- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n2601\t- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n2602\t- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n2603\t- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n2604\t- `sustain?: number`\n2605\t- `decay?: number`\n2606\t- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n2607\t- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n2608\t- `arpRate?: number`\n2609\t- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n2610\t- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n2611\t- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n2612\t- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n2613\t\n2614\t### interface Spatial\n2615\t\n2616\t- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n2617\t- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n2618\t- `distance: number`\n2619\t\n2620\t### spatialize()\n2621\t\n2622\t```ts\n2623\tfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n2624\t```\n2625\t\n2626\tGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n2627\t\n2628\t### interface SpatialOptions\n2629\t\n2630\t- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n2631\t- `max?: number`: Silent from this distance on (default 30); it fades out over the last fifth.\n2632\t- `rolloff?: number`: How fast it falls off past `ref` (default 1: inverse distance, the way sound really does).\n2633\t\n2634\t### interface Voice\n2635\t\n2636\tA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n2637\t\n2638\t- `readonly name: string`\n2639\t- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n2640\t- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n2641\t- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n2642\t- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n2643\t- `stop(): void`\n2644\t\n2645\t### interface VoiceSettings\n2646\t\n2647\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n2648\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n2649\t- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n2650\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n2651\t\n2652\t### type VolumeChannel\n2653\t\n2654\t```ts\n2655\ttype VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music'\n2656\t```\n2657\t\n2658\t### type Wave\n2659\t\n2660\t```ts\n2661\ttype Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise'\n2662\t```\n2663\t\n2664\tRetro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)\n2665\trendered to samples once, then played as a buffer. Deterministic (seeded noise), so\n2666\tsounds are unit-testable and identical on every machine.\n2667\t\n2668\t## 9. Particles\n2669\t\n2670\t### interface BurstOptions\n2671\t\n2672\t- `count?: number`: Override the particle count.\n2673\t- `direction?: Vec3`: Override the launch direction (e.g. opposite to a skid).\n2674\t- `speed?: number`: Multiply launch speed.\n2675\t- `scale?: number`: Multiply size.\n2676\t- `colors?: readonly PaletteColor[]`: Override the colors for this burst.\n2677\t\n2678\t### class ParticlePool\n2679\t\n2680\tFixed-size particle storage with CPU simulation. Alive particles are packed at the\n2681\tfront (swap-remove), so the GPU draws exactly `alive` instances from one buffer.\n2682\tPure (no three.js): unit-tested.\n2683\t\n2684\t- `new ParticlePool(preset: ParticlePreset)`\n2685\t- `readonly max: number`\n2686\t- `alive: number`\n2687\t- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n2688\t- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n2689\t- `readonly age: Float32Array`\n2690\t- `readonly life: Float32Array`\n2691\t- `readonly size0: Float32Array`\n2692\t- `readonly size1: Float32Array`\n2693\t- `readonly colorSet: Uint8Array`: Index into `colorSets` per particle.\n2694\t- `readonly colorSets: (readonly PaletteColor[])[]`: Color lists in use: [0] is the preset's, bursts with custom colors add more.\n2695\t- `readonly preset: ParticlePreset`\n2696\t- `spawn(at: Vec3, o: BurstOptions = {}, random: () => number = this.ownRandom): number`: Spawn a burst at `at`. Returns how many particles were spawned (the pool may be full). `random` in [0, 1): seeded, so bursts repeat.\n2697\t- `step(dt: number): void`: Advance every particle by `dt` seconds and drop the dead ones.\n2698\t- `t(i: number): number`: Life fraction 0..1 of particle `i`.\n2699\t- `sizeAt(i: number): number`: Size in whole art pixels (0 = invisible).\n2700\t- `colorAt(i: number): PaletteColor`: Current palette color name: stepped through the particle's color list.\n2701\t- `clear(): void`\n2702\t\n2703\t### interface ParticlePreset\n2704\t\n2705\tA particle effect as data. Sizes are in art pixels (whole pixels, so particles stay crisp\n2706\tsquares), colors are palette names stepped over each particle's life (no blending).\n2707\t\n2708\t- `count: Range`: Particles per burst.\n2709\t- `life: Range`: Seconds each particle lives.\n2710\t- `speed: Range`: Launch speed, world units / s.\n2711\t- `direction?: Vec3`: Main launch direction (default straight up).\n2712\t- `spread?: number`: Cone half-angle around `direction`, degrees (180 = every direction). Default 180.\n2713\t- `flatten?: number`: Multiply vertical launch speed (0.2 = dust that hugs the ground). Default 1.\n2714\t- `gravity?: number`: Downward acceleration, world units / s² (negative floats up). Default 0.\n2715\t- `drag?: number`: Velocity damping per second (0 = none).\n2716\t- `size: readonly [number, number]`: Size in art pixels at birth and at death (rounded to whole pixels).\n2717\t- `colors: readonly PaletteColor[]`: Palette colors stepped through over the lifetime.\n2718\t- `radius?: number`: Random spawn offset radius, world units.\n2719\t- `max?: number`: Pool size (max alive at once). Default 256.\n2720\t\n2721\t### type ParticlePresetName\n2722\t\n2723\t```ts\n2724\ttype ParticlePresetName = keyof typeof PARTICLES\n2725\t```\n2726\t\n2727\t### class Particles\n2728\t\n2729\tPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\n2730\tpalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\n2731\tpreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\n2732\tAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n2733\t(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n2734\t\n2735\t  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n2736\t  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n2737\t\n2738\t- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n2739\t- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n2740\t- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n2741\t- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n2742\t- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n2743\t- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\n2744\t- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n2745\t- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n2746\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n2747\t- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n2748\t- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n2749\t- `dispose(): void`\n2750\t\n2751\t### PARTICLES\n2752\t\n2753\t```ts\n2754\tconst PARTICLES: { dust: { count: [number, number]; life: [number, number]; speed: [number, number]; spread: number; flatten: number; gravity: number; drag: number; size: [number, number]; colors: (\"white\" | \"mist\" | \"slate\")[]; radius: number; }; skid: { ...; }; sparkle: { ...; }; smoke: { ...; }; impact: { ...; }; }\n2755\t```\n2756\t\n2757\tBuilt-in particle effects, as data (see ParticlePreset). Register more with\n2758\t`ctx.particles.register(name, preset)`. Sizes are art pixels; colors are PALETTE names.\n2759\t\n2760\t## 10. HUD\n2761\t\n2762\t### class Hud\n2763\t\n2764\tPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\n2765\tthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\n2766\tlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n2767\t(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n2768\t\n2769\tRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n2770\t(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\n2771\tresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n2772\t\n2773\t  ctx.hud.clear();\n2774\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n2775\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n2776\t\n2777\t- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n2778\t- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n2779\t- `visible: boolean`\n2780\t- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n2781\t- `get height: number`\n2782\t- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n2783\t- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n2784\t- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n2785\t- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n2786\t- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n2787\t- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n2788\t- `dispose(): void`\n2789\t\n2790\t### type HudAnchor\n2791\t\n2792\t```ts\n2793\ttype HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right'\n2794\t```\n2795\t\n2796\tWhere an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left).\n2797\t\n2798\t### type HudColor\n2799\t\n2800\t```ts\n2801\ttype HudColor = PaletteColor | number\n2802\t```\n2803\t\n2804\t### interface HudOptions\n2805\t\n2806\t- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n2807\t- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n2808\t- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n2809\t- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n2810\t\n2811\t### hudPlace()\n2812\t\n2813\t```ts\n2814\tfunction hudPlace(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, anchor: HudAnchor, size: Resolution): [number, number]\n2815\t```\n2816\t\n2817\tTop-left corner, in whole art pixels, of a w×h element placed at offset (x, y) from\n2818\t`anchor` on a screen of `size` art pixels. Pure (unit-tested).\n2819\t\n2820\t## 11. Models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n2821\t\n2822\t### addModelColliders()\n2823\t\n2824\t```ts\n2825\tfunction addModelColliders(physics: Physics, build: Pick<ModelBuild, 'colliders'>, at: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0], yaw = 0): RAPIER.Collider[]\n2826\t```\n2827\t\n2828\tStatic Rapier colliders for a placed model (its `colliders` data), at `at` turned `yaw`\n2829\tradians about Y: a box, an upright cylinder or a convex hull, with `addStaticBox`'s friction."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9dbc4055-18d5-4f5b-aa46-86fd0623cd25","uuid":"687b45f1-4957-427a-a9f7-9bf46f6b58b1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:22:02.794Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211944__x51597c/work/.research/engine/unpacked/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"## 7. Input and touch\n\n### applyDeadzone()\n\n```ts\nfunction applyDeadzone(x: number, y: number, deadzone = 0.2): { x: number; y: number }\n```\n\nRadial deadzone with rescaling: inside `deadzone` reads 0, the rim reads 1, and the\ndirection is kept (no square-gate snapping to the axes).\n\n### interface ButtonEvent\n\nA pointer button pressed or let go (`Input.buttonEvents`): its key, and where the pointer was (normalised device coordinates).\n\n- `readonly code: string`\n- `readonly down: boolean`\n- `readonly x: number`\n- `readonly y: number`\n\n### DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS\n\n```ts\nconst DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS: readonly TouchButton[]\n```\n\nDefault buttons for PlatformerCharacter (see character/controls.ts KEYMAP).\n\n### GAMEPAD_BUTTONS\n\n```ts\nconst GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>\n```\n\nStandard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\nKEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \"standard\" layout:\n0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\n\n### interface GamepadLike\n\nThe parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects).\n\n- `readonly connected?: boolean`\n- `readonly mapping?: string`\n- `readonly axes: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly buttons: readonly { readonly pressed: boolean; readonly value?: number }[]`\n\n### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n\n### interface InputFrame\n\nWhat the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\nOptional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\nfrom the frame before.\n\n- `dt: number`: Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it).\n- `held?: string[]`: Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted.\n- `pressed?: string[]`: Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held).\n- `analog?: [number, number]`: The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone).\n- `pad?: [number, number]`\n- `delta?: [number, number]`: Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame.\n- `wheel?: number`\n- `pointer?: [number, number, number, number]`: Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says).\n- `gamepad?: boolean`: Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes.\n- `touch?: boolean`: Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes.\n- `clicks?: [string, 0 | 1, number, number][]`: Pointer buttons pressed (1) and let go (0) this frame, in order, each where the pointer was (`buttonEvents`).\n- `commands?: unknown[]`: Data from outside the canvas delivered this frame (`send()`): a DOM widget's value, a save read from storage.\n- `sync?: true`: Ran in the same task as the frame before (frames of one `step(n)` call): no promise callbacks ran between them. A replay waits for them before every other frame.\n\n### interface InputState\n\nWhat input a later frame still reads, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): keys held, presses not yet used and how old they are, the pointer.\n\n- `readonly held: readonly string[]`\n- `readonly queued: readonly (readonly [string, number])[]`: Queued presses: [key, seconds of game time since it was pressed].\n- `readonly pointer: readonly [number, number, number, number]`\n- `readonly gamepad: boolean`\n- `readonly outbox: readonly unknown[]`: Data sent (`send`) and not yet delivered: the next frame that runs delivers it.\n\n### MOUSE_BUTTONS\n\n```ts\nconst MOUSE_BUTTONS: readonly (readonly [code: string, bit: number])[]\n```\n\nMouse buttons as key codes (`isDown('MouseLeft')`, `consumePress('MouseRight')`), each with\nits `PointerEvent.buttons` bit, by `PointerEvent.button` (0 left, 1 middle, 2 right).\n\n### PRESS_WINDOW\n\n```ts\nconst PRESS_WINDOW: 0.15\n```\n\nHow long a queued press stays consumable, in seconds of game time.\n\n### TOUCH_KEY\n\n```ts\nconst TOUCH_KEY: \"Touch\"\n```\n\nA finger on the canvas, as a key: held while one is down (not a mouse button, so a tap is not a click).\n\n### interface TouchButton\n\n- `label: string`\n- `code: string`: Key code this button presses (e.g. 'Space').\n- `hint?: string`: Short hint shown under the label.\n- `at?: { x: number; y: number; size?: number; corner?: TouchCorner }`: Place the button yourself instead of in the default grid: its centre, in CSS pixels from `corner` (default bottom-right; `x` grows inward from the side, `y` inward from the top or bottom edge), and its diameter (`size`, default 58). An action game's cluster (…\n\n### interface TouchButtonState\n\nWhat a button shows right now (`TouchControls.set`): a game drives its buttons from its own\nstate each frame (skill icons, cooldowns, costs, a contextual action). Only what changed\ntouches the DOM.\n\n- `label?: string`\n- `hint?: string`\n- `icon?: HTMLCanvasElement | HTMLImageElement | null`: A picture instead of the label (a canvas or image; drawn pixelated); null removes it.\n- `cooldown?: number`: Cooldown shutter, 0..1 of the button covered (0 = ready).\n- `timer?: string`: Text over the shutter (seconds left).\n- `badge?: string`: A small corner badge (a cost, a count).\n- `disabled?: boolean`: Dimmed (can't be used now); it still presses its key.\n- `hidden?: boolean`: Not shown (its place is kept), and its key is released.\n\n### class TouchControls\n\nOn-screen controls for phones and tablets: a virtual joystick (bottom-left) that feeds\n`input.analog`, action buttons (bottom-right, in a grid or placed one by one with `at`)\nthat press key codes, and a small top bar (debug panel, Pixel/Raw, resolution, looks).\nCamera: drag on the game to orbit/look, pinch to zoom (handled by Input.attachPointer).\nShown automatically on coarse pointers, or with `?touch=1`.\n\nA game drives it at runtime: `set(code, state)` (icon, cooldown, badge, disabled, hidden),\n`show(false)` while a menu owns the screen, and `rects()` to lay its HUD out around the\ncontrols.\n\n- `new TouchControls(container: HTMLElement, input: Input, buttons: readonly TouchButton[] = DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS, topBar: readonly TouchButton[] = [])`\n- `static wanted(search = location.search): boolean`\n- `readonly root: HTMLDivElement`\n- `set(code: string, state: TouchButtonState): void`: Update every button that presses `code` (see TouchButtonState).\n- `show(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the joystick and the action buttons (the top bar stays): hide them while a menu or a full-screen view owns the screen. Hiding releases every held key and the stick.\n- `get visible: boolean`\n- `rects(): { stick: DOMRect | null; buttons: { code: string; label: string; hidden: boolean; rect: DOMRect }[]; bar: DOMRect | null }`: Client rects (CSS pixels) of the controls on screen now: the joystick and every button, with the key code it presses. A hidden button keeps its place (`hidden` says it isn't drawn), so a HUD laid out around them doesn't move when a contextual button comes and…\n\n### type TouchCorner\n\n```ts\ntype TouchCorner = 'bottom-right' | 'bottom-left' | 'top-right' | 'top-left'\n```\n\nCorner of the screen a placed touch button is measured from (inside the safe area).\n\n## 8. Audio\n\n### attenuation()\n\n```ts\nfunction attenuation(distance: number, o: SpatialOptions = {}): number\n```\n\nVolume at `distance` (m): 1 inside `ref`, then inverse distance, fading to 0 at `max`.\n\n### class AudioManager\n\nWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\nsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\npersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n\nBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\nfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\nwas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n\n- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n- `context: AudioContext | null`\n- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n- `get muted: boolean`\n- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n### interface AudioSettings\n\n- `master: number`\n- `sfx: number`\n- `music: number`\n- `muted: boolean`\n\n### type Instrument\n\n```ts\ntype Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>\n```\n\nA track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those).\n\n### interface Listener\n\n- `position: V3Like`\n- `right: V3Like`: The listener's right, in world space (unit): the camera's right for a third-person game.\n\n### muffleCutoff\n\n```ts\nconst muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number\n```\n\nLow-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud.\n\n### noteFrequency()\n\n```ts\nfunction noteFrequency(note: string): number | null\n```\n\n'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note.\n\n### parseSong()\n\n```ts\nfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n```\n\nFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n\n### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n\n```ts\nconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n```\n\nThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n\n### interface PlayOptions\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n\n### renderSound()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n```\n\nRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n\n### SFX\n\n```ts\nconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n```\n\nBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\nSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n\n### type SfxName\n\n```ts\ntype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n```\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n### interface Spatial\n\n- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `distance: number`\n\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n### interface SpatialOptions\n\n- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n- `max?: number`: Silent from this distance on (default 30); it fades out over the last fifth.\n- `rolloff?: number`: How fast it falls off past `ref` (default 1: inverse distance, the way sound really does).\n\n### interface Voice\n\nA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n- `stop(): void`\n\n### interface VoiceSettings\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n\n### type VolumeChannel\n\n```ts\ntype VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music'\n```\n\n### type Wave\n\n```ts\ntype Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise'\n```\n\nRetro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)\nrendered to samples once, then played as a buffer. Deterministic (seeded noise), so\nsounds are unit-testable and identical on every machine.\n\n## 9. Particles\n\n### interface BurstOptions\n\n- `count?: number`: Override the particle count.\n- `direction?: Vec3`: Override the launch direction (e.g. opposite to a skid).\n- `speed?: number`: Multiply launch speed.\n- `scale?: number`: Multiply size.\n- `colors?: readonly PaletteColor[]`: Override the colors for this burst.\n\n### class ParticlePool\n\nFixed-size particle storage with CPU simulation. Alive particles are packed at the\nfront (swap-remove), so the GPU draws exactly `alive` instances from one buffer.\nPure (no three.js): unit-tested.\n\n- `new ParticlePool(preset: ParticlePreset)`\n- `readonly max: number`\n- `alive: number`\n- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n- `readonly age: Float32Array`\n- `readonly life: Float32Array`\n- `readonly size0: Float32Array`\n- `readonly size1: Float32Array`\n- `readonly colorSet: Uint8Array`: Index into `colorSets` per particle.\n- `readonly colorSets: (readonly PaletteColor[])[]`: Color lists in use: [0] is the preset's, bursts with custom colors add more.\n- `readonly preset: ParticlePreset`\n- `spawn(at: Vec3, o: BurstOptions = {}, random: () => number = this.ownRandom): number`: Spawn a burst at `at`. Returns how many particles were spawned (the pool may be full). `random` in [0, 1): seeded, so bursts repeat.\n- `step(dt: number): void`: Advance every particle by `dt` seconds and drop the dead ones.\n- `t(i: number): number`: Life fraction 0..1 of particle `i`.\n- `sizeAt(i: number): number`: Size in whole art pixels (0 = invisible).\n- `colorAt(i: number): PaletteColor`: Current palette color name: stepped through the particle's color list.\n- `clear(): void`\n\n### interface ParticlePreset\n\nA particle effect as data. Sizes are in art pixels (whole pixels, so particles stay crisp\nsquares), colors are palette names stepped over each particle's life (no blending).\n\n- `count: Range`: Particles per burst.\n- `life: Range`: Seconds each particle lives.\n- `speed: Range`: Launch speed, world units / s.\n- `direction?: Vec3`: Main launch direction (default straight up).\n- `spread?: number`: Cone half-angle around `direction`, degrees (180 = every direction). Default 180.\n- `flatten?: number`: Multiply vertical launch speed (0.2 = dust that hugs the ground). Default 1.\n- `gravity?: number`: Downward acceleration, world units / s² (negative floats up). Default 0.\n- `drag?: number`: Velocity damping per second (0 = none).\n- `size: readonly [number, number]`: Size in art pixels at birth and at death (rounded to whole pixels).\n- `colors: readonly PaletteColor[]`: Palette colors stepped through over the lifetime.\n- `radius?: number`: Random spawn offset radius, world units.\n- `max?: number`: Pool size (max alive at once). Default 256.\n\n### type ParticlePresetName\n\n```ts\ntype ParticlePresetName = keyof typeof PARTICLES\n```\n\n### class Particles\n\nPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\npalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\npreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\nAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n\n  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n\n- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\n- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### PARTICLES\n\n```ts\nconst PARTICLES: { dust: { count: [number, number]; life: [number, number]; speed: [number, number]; spread: number; flatten: number; gravity: number; drag: number; size: [number, number]; colors: (\"white\" | \"mist\" | \"slate\")[]; radius: number; }; skid: { ...; }; sparkle: { ...; }; smoke: { ...; }; impact: { ...; }; }\n```\n\nBuilt-in particle effects, as data (see ParticlePreset). Register more with\n`ctx.particles.register(name, preset)`. Sizes are art pixels; colors are PALETTE names.\n\n## 10. HUD\n\n### class Hud\n\nPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\nthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\nlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n\nRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\nresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n\n  ctx.hud.clear();\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n\n- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n- `visible: boolean`\n- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n- `get height: number`\n- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### type HudAnchor\n\n```ts\ntype HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right'\n```\n\nWhere an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left).\n\n### type HudColor\n\n```ts\ntype HudColor = PaletteColor | number\n```\n\n### interface HudOptions\n\n- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n\n### hudPlace()\n\n```ts\nfunction hudPlace(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, anchor: HudAnchor, size: Resolution): [number, number]\n```\n\nTop-left corner, in whole art pixels, of a w×h element placed at offset (x, y) from\n`anchor` on a screen of `size` art pixels. Pure (unit-tested).\n\n## 11. Models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n\n### addModelColliders()\n\n```ts\nfunction addModelColliders(physics: Physics, build: Pick<ModelBuild, 'colliders'>, at: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0], yaw = 0): RAPIER.Collider[]\n```\n\nStatic Rapier colliders for a placed model (its `colliders` data), at `at` turned `yaw`\nradians about Y: a box, an upright cylinder or a convex hull, with `addStaticBox`'s friction.","numLines":590,"startLine":2240,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014zF7gZ5XWW2k9B8qJ4joXb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9Av8mhiwP3HZ5VGfEE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KZapUKjPV5TyfkceWPG7TW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211944__x51597c/work/.research/engine/unpacked/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":3539,"limit":360},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27079,"cache_read_input_tokens":114192,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27079},"output_tokens":75,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9dbc4055-18d5-4f5b-aa46-86fd0623cd25","uuid":"f7916fef-04f1-4f65-9ac3-ba5f342d454f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:22:04.425Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9Austm3yRk6NtSE1TU","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KZapUKjPV5TyfkceWPG7TW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211944__x51597c/work/.research/engine/unpacked/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":3539,"limit":360}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KZapUKjPV5TyfkceWPG7TW","type":"tool_result","content":"3539\t## 12. Animation toolkit\n3540\t\n3541\t### analyzeClip()\n3542\t\n3543\t```ts\n3544\tfunction analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport\n3545\t```\n3546\t\n3547\t### interface ClipDef\n3548\t\n3549\t- `name: string`\n3550\t- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n3551\t- `loop?: boolean`\n3552\t- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n3553\t- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n3554\t- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n3555\t- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n3556\t- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n3557\t- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n3558\t- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n3559\t- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n3560\t- `notes?: string`\n3561\t\n3562\t### interface ClipReport\n3563\t\n3564\tObjective checks for a clip, computed by actually posing the rig (no renderer). These\n3565\tare the numbers an agent can't eyeball: foot sliding, ground contact, loop seams,\n3566\tvelocity spikes, joint limits. Thresholds are deliberately forgiving; `problems` are\n3567\treal defects, `warnings` are worth a look.\n3568\t\n3569\t- `name: string`\n3570\t- `frames: number`\n3571\t- `loop: boolean`\n3572\t- `speed: number | null`\n3573\t- `minSoleY: number`: Lowest sole point over the clip (m) and the frame it happens. Negative = through the floor.\n3574\t- `minSoleFrame: number`\n3575\t- `minBodyY: number`: Lowest point of any other body part (knees, hands, head...), where and when.\n3576\t- `minBodyMesh: string`\n3577\t- `minBodyFrame: number`\n3578\t- `floatingFrames: number`: Frames (of grounded clips) where neither sole is within 5 cm of the floor.\n3579\t- `footSlide: number`: Mean horizontal slip of the planted foot, m/s (0 = perfectly planted).\n3580\t- `loopSeam: number`: Largest joint-angle jump between the last and first frame of a loop, degrees.\n3581\t- `maxAngularSpeed: { joint: string; degPerSec: number; frame: number }`: Fastest joint rotation, degrees per second, and where.\n3582\t- `limitViolations: { joint: string; axis: 'x' | 'y' | 'z'; frame: number; value: number; range: [number, number] }[]`\n3583\t- `pelvisY: [number, number]`: Pelvis height range (m) — how much the body bobs.\n3584\t- `problems: string[]`\n3585\t- `warnings: string[]`\n3586\t\n3587\t### compileClip()\n3588\t\n3589\t```ts\n3590\tfunction compileClip(def: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec, rest: RestPose): AnimationClip\n3591\t```\n3592\t\n3593\tBake a ClipDef into a three.js AnimationClip (quaternion + position + scale tracks,\n3594\tsampled at 60 Hz so easing and per-axis Euler interpolation — including 360° flips —\n3595\tsurvive exactly).\n3596\t\n3597\t### compileClips()\n3598\t\n3599\t```ts\n3600\tfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n3601\t```\n3602\t\n3603\t### interface Foot\n3604\t\n3605\t- `readonly at: Vec3`: World position of the foot now.\n3606\t- `t: number`: Stepping: 0 → 1 through the step; -1 planted.\n3607\t- `readonly from: Vec3`\n3608\t- `readonly to: Vec3`\n3609\t\n3610\t### interface FootGoal\n3611\t\n3612\tFoot-placement IK: say where a foot should be, get the hip/knee/ankle rotations.\n3613\t\n3614\tAuthors write the body (pelvis height, lean, arms...) and then pin the feet:\n3615\t\n3616\t  placeFeet({ Pelvis: { p: [0, -0.2, 0] }, Torso: [20, 0, 0] }, rig, {\n3617\t    R: { z: 0.15 },                             // right foot flat, 15 cm ahead\n3618\t    L: { z: -0.2, pitch: 30, pivot: 'ball' },   // left heel raised on its toes\n3619\t  })\n3620\t\n3621\tCoordinates are the character's ground frame: y up from the floor, z forward. The solve\n3622\tis planar (legs bend about X, knees forward); lateral offsets are ignored. It accounts\n3623\tfor the root's position, rotation and squash, so a squashed or leaning body still\n3624\tkeeps its feet exactly where they were asked…\n3625\t\n3626\t- `z: number`: Forward position of the ankle (m) when the foot is flat.\n3627\t- `x?: number`: Sideways position (m, + = the character's left). Default: under the hip at rest. Only matters when the root is turned (e.g. a pivot on one foot); the solve itself stays in the leg's plane.\n3628\t- `y?: number`: Height of the sole above the floor (m). 0 = standing on it.\n3629\t- `pitch?: number`: Sole pitch in degrees: + = toes down / heel raised, − = toes up.\n3630\t- `pivot?: 'heel' | 'ball' | 'ankle'`: Point that stays at (z, y) while pitching: heel (heel strike), toe tip (tiptoe, toe-off) or ankle.\n3631\t- `follow?: number`: 0..1: let the foot hang from the shin instead of holding its world pitch (swinging feet, dangling in the air). 1 = foot at `relax` degrees relative to the shin.\n3632\t- `relax?: number`: Foot angle relative to the shin when following (default 15 = toes slightly down).\n3633\t\n3634\t### gaitClip()\n3635\t\n3636\t```ts\n3637\tfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n3638\t```\n3639\t\n3640\t### gaitPartners()\n3641\t\n3642\t```ts\n3643\tfunction gaitPartners(n: number): number[][]\n3644\t```\n3645\t\n3646\tPartners for `n` legs in two rows (left 0, 2, 4…, right 1, 3, 5…): a leg waits for the legs beside and opposite it.\n3647\t\n3648\t### interface GaitSpec\n3649\t\n3650\tProcedural locomotion cycles with planted feet. Instead of posing every frame, describe\n3651\tthe gait (speed, cadence, stance, bounce, arm swing...) and the generator plants each\n3652\tfoot on the ground, sliding it backwards at exactly `speed` while it is in stance, then\n3653\tswings it forward on an arc — heel strike, roll, toe-off included. Tweak numbers,\n3654\tre-run `npm run anim -- check Walk`, look at the sheet.\n3655\t\n3656\tPhase: the RIGHT heel strikes at frame 0, the left at frames / 2.\n3657\t\n3658\t- `name: string`\n3659\t- `frames: number`: One full cycle (two steps), frames at 30 fps.\n3660\t- `speed: number`: Ground speed the cycle is authored for (m/s at playback rate 1).\n3661\t- `stance: number`: Fraction of the cycle each foot is on the ground. Walk ≈ 0.55–0.6, run ≈ 0.3.\n3662\t- `hip: number`: Mean pelvis height offset from rest (m); negative bends the knees.\n3663\t- `bob?: number`: Pelvis bounce amplitude (m), twice per cycle.\n3664\t- `low?: number`: Cycle fraction at which the pelvis is lowest (default: walk just after contact, run mid-stance).\n3665\t- `squash?: number`: Squash at the lowest point (e.g. 0.05 = 5 % shorter, wider).\n3666\t- `lift?: number`: How high the lowest point of the swinging foot clears the floor at its peak (m).\n3667\t- `liftPeak?: number`: Where in the swing (0..1) the foot is highest (default 0.4).\n3668\t- `reach?: number`: Shift the whole stride forward (m).\n3669\t- `swingReach?: number`: Extra forward throw of the swinging foot late in the swing, before it settles (m).\n3670\t- `swingDelay?: number`: Swing timing: 0 = even, + = the foot hangs back then whips forward (running).\n3671\t- `plant?: number`: 0..1: how much the swinging foot sweeps back to meet the ground at touchdown. At 1 it arrives moving backwards at exactly the ground speed (still, in the world), so the foot plants without sliding; at 0 it eases to a stop relative to the hips and slides forwa…\n3672\t- `heelStrike?: number`: Toes-up angle at heel strike (deg).\n3673\t- `toeOff?: number`: Heel-raise angle at toe-off (deg).\n3674\t- `roll?: [heel: number, toe: number]`: Fraction of stance spent rolling off the heel / onto the toes.\n3675\t- `tiptoe?: number`: Walk on the balls of the feet: extra pitch through stance (deg), pivoting on the ball.\n3676\t- `swingPitch?: number`: Extra toes-down droop mid-swing (deg).\n3677\t- `dangle?: number`: How much the swinging foot hangs from the shin mid-swing (0..1, default 0.8).\n3678\t- `lean?: number`: Torso lean forward (deg) and twist of the shoulders against the legs (deg).\n3679\t- `twist?: number`\n3680\t- `sway?: number`: Side-to-side torso roll (deg).\n3681\t- `pelvisPitch?: number`: Pelvis pitch (deg): tips the whole body, legs are re-solved.\n3682\t- `head?: number`: Head pitch (deg) and how much it counters the torso twist (0..1).\n3683\t- `headSteady?: number`\n3684\t- `arms?: { /** Swing amplitude (deg). */ swing: number; /** Base elbow bend (deg). */ elbow: number; /** Extra elbow bend when the arm is forward (deg). */ pump?: numbe…`\n3685\t- `pose?: Pose`: Extra joints/overrides mixed into every frame (hands, hat...).\n3686\t- `extra?: (u: number, pose: Record<string, Euler | JointPose>) => void`: Per-frame hook for anything else: receives the cycle phase 0..1.\n3687\t- `step?: number`: Key spacing in frames (default 0.5 — IK is non-linear, keep it fine).\n3688\t- `notes?: string`\n3689\t\n3690\t### type Key\n3691\t\n3692\t```ts\n3693\ttype Key = readonly [number, Pose, Ease?]\n3694\t```\n3695\t\n3696\t`[frame, pose, easeToNextKey?]`\n3697\t\n3698\t### interface LegDef\n3699\t\n3700\t- `rest: readonly [number, number]`: Where the foot rests, in the body's space (x right, z forward; y ignored).\n3701\t- `partners?: readonly number[]`: Legs that must not be lifting when this one starts a step (its neighbours; mutual, so listing a pair once is enough).\n3702\t\n3703\t### class LegStepper\n3704\t\n3705\tFeet for a body with any number of legs. `update(dt, body, yaw, velocity, ground)`:\n3706\tthe body's world position and heading, how fast it moves, and the ground height at a point.\n3707\t\n3708\t- `new LegStepper(o: LegStepperOptions)`\n3709\t- `readonly feet: Foot[]`\n3710\t- `o: Required<Omit<LegStepperOptions, 'legs'>> & { legs: readonly LegDef[] }`\n3711\t- `steps: number`: Steps started (tests).\n3712\t- `target(i: number, body: Vec3, yaw: number, velocity: Vec3, ground: (x: number, z: number) => number, out: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0]): Vec3`: Where leg `i` wants to be: its rest spot around the body, ahead by the lead, on the ground.\n3713\t- `reset(body: Vec3, yaw: number, ground: (x: number, z: number) => number): void`: Every foot planted on its rest spot around `body` (a spawn or a teleport). The first `update` does this itself.\n3714\t- `update(dt: number, body: Vec3, yaw: number, velocity: Vec3, ground: (x: number, z: number) => number): void`\n3715\t- `planted(): number`: Feet on the ground now.\n3716\t\n3717\t### interface LegStepperOptions\n3718\t\n3719\t- `legs: readonly LegDef[]`\n3720\t- `threshold?: number`: Step when the foot is this far from its target (m). Default 0.3.\n3721\t- `stepTime?: number`: Seconds a step takes. Default 0.18.\n3722\t- `lift?: number`: How high a step lifts the foot (m). Default 0.15.\n3723\t- `lead?: number`: Aim ahead of the body's motion by this many seconds (feet land where it is going). Default 0.25.\n3724\t\n3725\t### mirror()\n3726\t\n3727\t```ts\n3728\tfunction mirror(pose: Pose, rig: Pick<RigSpec, 'mirror'>): Pose\n3729\t```\n3730\t\n3731\tMirror a pose left ↔ right (rig.mirror pairs; Y/Z rotations and X offsets flip).\n3732\t\n3733\t### placeFeet()\n3734\t\n3735\t```ts\n3736\tfunction placeFeet(pose: Pose, rig: RigSpec, goals: Partial<Record<Side, FootGoal>>): Pose\n3737\t```\n3738\t\n3739\tReturn `pose` with its legs solved so the feet land on the goals. Existing Y/Z\n3740\trotations on the leg joints (spread, twist) are kept; X is replaced.\n3741\t\n3742\t### type Pose\n3743\t\n3744\t```ts\n3745\ttype Pose = Readonly<Record<string, Euler | JointPose>>\n3746\t```\n3747\t\n3748\tJoint name → rotation (degrees) or full joint pose. Missing joints are at rest.\n3749\t\n3750\t### renderCurves()\n3751\t\n3752\t```ts\n3753\tfunction renderCurves(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip, options: CurveOptions = {}): SheetImage\n3754\t```\n3755\t\n3756\t### renderSheet()\n3757\t\n3758\t```ts\n3759\tfunction renderSheet(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip, options: SheetOptions = {}): SheetImage\n3760\t```\n3761\t\n3762\tRender a contact sheet for one clip.\n3763\t\n3764\t### interface RigSpec\n3765\t\n3766\tEverything the tools need to know about a rig beyond its hierarchy.\n3767\t\n3768\t- `joints: readonly string[]`: Joint names in hierarchy order (root first).\n3769\t- `root: string`\n3770\t- `mirror: Readonly<Record<string, string>>`: Left/right counterparts for mirroring.\n3771\t- `soles: readonly string[]`: Mesh names that touch the ground (soles).\n3772\t- `trace: readonly string[]`: Points to trace in contact sheets (joint or mesh names).\n3773\t- `limits: Readonly<Record<string, JointLimit>>`: Human-plausible rotation ranges, degrees.\n3774\t- `fps: number`: Frame rate of the authoring format.\n3775\t- `legs?: LegRig`: Leg geometry, enabling foot IK and the gait generator.\n3776\t- `spine?: { torso?: string; head?: string; cap?: string }`: Joints the runtime procedural layers turn (PoseLayers): the torso and head look where the character is going; `cap` is an optional springy joint (not animated by clips).\n3777\t\n3778\t### sampleClip()\n3779\t\n3780\t```ts\n3781\tfunction sampleClip(clip: ClipDef, frame: number, rig: RigSpec | readonly string[]): Record<string, ResolvedJoint>\n3782\t```\n3783\t\n3784\tEvaluate a clip's pose at `frame` (keys + layers + feet track), fully resolved for every\n3785\tjoint of `rig` (or the listed joints — then the feet track needs a rig and is skipped).\n3786\t\n3787\t### interface SheetImage\n3788\t\n3789\tTiny RGBA software canvas shared by contact sheets, curve plots and filmstrips (no GPU, no DOM).\n3790\t\n3791\t- `width: number`\n3792\t- `height: number`\n3793\t- `data: Uint8ClampedArray`\n3794\t\n3795\t### class SpringChain\n3796\t\n3797\tA chain of points hanging off a moving root. Call `update(dt, root, basis)` every frame\n3798\twith the root's world position and its orientation (three columns: right, up, forward),\n3799\tthen read `points` (root first).\n3800\t\n3801\t- `new SpringChain(o: SpringChainOptions)`\n3802\t- `readonly points: Vec3[]`\n3803\t- `o: Required<SpringChainOptions>`\n3804\t- `reset(root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`: Put the chain straight out in its rest pose (no swing).\n3805\t- `update(dt: number, root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`\n3806\t- `swing(basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): number`: How far the tip is from where the rest pose puts it (m): how much it is swinging.\n3807\t\n3808\t### interface SpringChainOptions\n3809\t\n3810\t- `segments: number`: Points after the root (≥ 1).\n3811\t- `length: number`: Length of each segment (m).\n3812\t- `rest?: Vec3`: Rest direction from the root in the root's own space (normalised; default straight back, −Z).\n3813\t- `stiffness?: number`: Pull toward the rest pose per second (0 floppy … 30 stiff). Default 10.\n3814\t- `keep?: number`: Velocity kept per 1/60 s (0..1). Default 0.94.\n3815\t- `gravity?: number`: Gravity on the chain (m/s²). Default 6 (lighter than real: tails hold up a bit).\n3816\t\n3817\t### class Squash\n3818\t\n3819\tSquash and stretch as a damped spring on `value` (0 = rest, + stretched, − squashed).\n3820\t`scale()` turns it into a volume-preserving pair: `const [h, v] = squash.scale();\n3821\tmesh.scale.set(h, v, h)`.\n3822\t\n3823\t- `value: number`\n3824\t- `velocity: number`\n3825\t- `stiffness: number`: Spring stiffness and damping (a critically damped spring has damping = 2√stiffness).\n3826\t- `damping: number`\n3827\t- `amount: number`: 0 turns it off; 2.5 is rubbery.\n3828\t- `kick(v: number): void`: Add a kick: negative squashes (a landing), positive stretches (a jump).\n3829\t- `set(v: number): void`: Set the shape directly (a held squash).\n3830\t- `update(dt: number): void`\n3831\t- `scale(out: [number, number] = [1, 1]): [number, number]`: [horizontal, vertical] scale: y = 1 + s, xz = 1 / √(1 + s), so the volume stays put.\n3832\t\n3833\t### twoBoneIK()\n3834\t\n3835\t```ts\n3836\tfunction twoBoneIK(root: Vec3, target: Vec3, a: number, b: number, pole: Vec3, out: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0]): Vec3\n3837\t```\n3838\t\n3839\tWhere the middle joint of a two-bone limb goes (a knee, an elbow) so the end reaches\n3840\t`target` from `root` with bones `a` and `b` long, bending toward `pole` (a point the knee\n3841\tpoints at). Analytic (the law of cosines), no iterations; a target out of reach straightens\n3842\tthe limb toward it, one too close folds it as far as it goes. Returns the joint, into `out`.\n3843\t\n3844\t### validateClip()\n3845\t\n3846\t```ts\n3847\tfunction validateClip(clip: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec): string[]\n3848\t```\n3849\t\n3850\tProblems with a clip definition itself (unknown joints, bad key order, loop mismatch).\n3851\t\n3852\t## 13. Hero kit\n3853\t\n3854\t### HERO_CLIPS\n3855\t\n3856\t```ts\n3857\tconst HERO_CLIPS: readonly ClipDef[]\n3858\t```\n3859\t\n3860\t### HERO_MODEL\n3861\t\n3862\t```ts\n3863\tconst HERO_MODEL: \"assets/hero.glb\"\n3864\t```\n3865\t\n3866\t### HERO_RAGDOLL\n3867\t\n3868\t```ts\n3869\tconst HERO_RAGDOLL: readonly RagdollPart[]\n3870\t```\n3871\t\n3872\tThe hero as a ragdoll (the joints of `hero.glb`, see rig.ts): pelvis, torso and head, upper\n3873\tarms and legs on ball joints held in cones, elbows and knees as hinges bending the way the\n3874\tclips bend them (Forearm −X, Shin +X). Hands and feet ride on their forearm and shin.\n3875\t\n3876\t### HERO_RIG\n3877\t\n3878\t```ts\n3879\tconst HERO_RIG: RigSpec\n3880\t```\n3881\t\n3882\tThe hero rig (public/assets/hero.glb, built by scripts/assets/hero.mjs).\n3883\t\n3884\tThe character faces +Z. L/R are the character's own sides (its right = -X).\n3885\tRotations are Euler XYZ degrees relative to rest; Z is applied first, then Y, then X,\n3886\tall about the parent's axes. Verified against contact sheets (`npm run anim -- sheet`).\n3887\t\n3888\t  Pelvis   +X tip forward (90 = face down, -90 = on its back, 360 = front flip)\n3889\t           +Y turn to its left, +Z roll to its RIGHT. p = [x, y, z] offset (m), s = squash\n3890\t  Torso    +X bend forward, +Y twist left, +Z lean right\n3891\t  Head     +X look down, +Y look left, +Z tilt right\n3892\t  Arm*     X swings: -90 = straight ahead, -180 = overhead, +40 = back\n3893\t           Z spreads (…\n3894\t\n3895\t## 14. three.js and Rapier\n3896\t\n3897\t### RAPIER\n3898\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9dbc4055-18d5-4f5b-aa46-86fd0623cd25","uuid":"5d52b6db-5ff6-465b-84f9-ca81f181bf59","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:22:04.443Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211944__x51597c/work/.research/engine/unpacked/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"## 12. Animation toolkit\n\n### analyzeClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport\n```\n\n### interface ClipDef\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n- `loop?: boolean`\n- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### interface ClipReport\n\nObjective checks for a clip, computed by actually posing the rig (no renderer). These\nare the numbers an agent can't eyeball: foot sliding, ground contact, loop seams,\nvelocity spikes, joint limits. Thresholds are deliberately forgiving; `problems` are\nreal defects, `warnings` are worth a look.\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`\n- `loop: boolean`\n- `speed: number | null`\n- `minSoleY: number`: Lowest sole point over the clip (m) and the frame it happens. Negative = through the floor.\n- `minSoleFrame: number`\n- `minBodyY: number`: Lowest point of any other body part (knees, hands, head...), where and when.\n- `minBodyMesh: string`\n- `minBodyFrame: number`\n- `floatingFrames: number`: Frames (of grounded clips) where neither sole is within 5 cm of the floor.\n- `footSlide: number`: Mean horizontal slip of the planted foot, m/s (0 = perfectly planted).\n- `loopSeam: number`: Largest joint-angle jump between the last and first frame of a loop, degrees.\n- `maxAngularSpeed: { joint: string; degPerSec: number; frame: number }`: Fastest joint rotation, degrees per second, and where.\n- `limitViolations: { joint: string; axis: 'x' | 'y' | 'z'; frame: number; value: number; range: [number, number] }[]`\n- `pelvisY: [number, number]`: Pelvis height range (m) — how much the body bobs.\n- `problems: string[]`\n- `warnings: string[]`\n\n### compileClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClip(def: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec, rest: RestPose): AnimationClip\n```\n\nBake a ClipDef into a three.js AnimationClip (quaternion + position + scale tracks,\nsampled at 60 Hz so easing and per-axis Euler interpolation — including 360° flips —\nsurvive exactly).\n\n### compileClips()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n```\n\n### interface Foot\n\n- `readonly at: Vec3`: World position of the foot now.\n- `t: number`: Stepping: 0 → 1 through the step; -1 planted.\n- `readonly from: Vec3`\n- `readonly to: Vec3`\n\n### interface FootGoal\n\nFoot-placement IK: say where a foot should be, get the hip/knee/ankle rotations.\n\nAuthors write the body (pelvis height, lean, arms...) and then pin the feet:\n\n  placeFeet({ Pelvis: { p: [0, -0.2, 0] }, Torso: [20, 0, 0] }, rig, {\n    R: { z: 0.15 },                             // right foot flat, 15 cm ahead\n    L: { z: -0.2, pitch: 30, pivot: 'ball' },   // left heel raised on its toes\n  })\n\nCoordinates are the character's ground frame: y up from the floor, z forward. The solve\nis planar (legs bend about X, knees forward); lateral offsets are ignored. It accounts\nfor the root's position, rotation and squash, so a squashed or leaning body still\nkeeps its feet exactly where they were asked…\n\n- `z: number`: Forward position of the ankle (m) when the foot is flat.\n- `x?: number`: Sideways position (m, + = the character's left). Default: under the hip at rest. Only matters when the root is turned (e.g. a pivot on one foot); the solve itself stays in the leg's plane.\n- `y?: number`: Height of the sole above the floor (m). 0 = standing on it.\n- `pitch?: number`: Sole pitch in degrees: + = toes down / heel raised, − = toes up.\n- `pivot?: 'heel' | 'ball' | 'ankle'`: Point that stays at (z, y) while pitching: heel (heel strike), toe tip (tiptoe, toe-off) or ankle.\n- `follow?: number`: 0..1: let the foot hang from the shin instead of holding its world pitch (swinging feet, dangling in the air). 1 = foot at `relax` degrees relative to the shin.\n- `relax?: number`: Foot angle relative to the shin when following (default 15 = toes slightly down).\n\n### gaitClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n```\n\n### gaitPartners()\n\n```ts\nfunction gaitPartners(n: number): number[][]\n```\n\nPartners for `n` legs in two rows (left 0, 2, 4…, right 1, 3, 5…): a leg waits for the legs beside and opposite it.\n\n### interface GaitSpec\n\nProcedural locomotion cycles with planted feet. Instead of posing every frame, describe\nthe gait (speed, cadence, stance, bounce, arm swing...) and the generator plants each\nfoot on the ground, sliding it backwards at exactly `speed` while it is in stance, then\nswings it forward on an arc — heel strike, roll, toe-off included. Tweak numbers,\nre-run `npm run anim -- check Walk`, look at the sheet.\n\nPhase: the RIGHT heel strikes at frame 0, the left at frames / 2.\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: One full cycle (two steps), frames at 30 fps.\n- `speed: number`: Ground speed the cycle is authored for (m/s at playback rate 1).\n- `stance: number`: Fraction of the cycle each foot is on the ground. Walk ≈ 0.55–0.6, run ≈ 0.3.\n- `hip: number`: Mean pelvis height offset from rest (m); negative bends the knees.\n- `bob?: number`: Pelvis bounce amplitude (m), twice per cycle.\n- `low?: number`: Cycle fraction at which the pelvis is lowest (default: walk just after contact, run mid-stance).\n- `squash?: number`: Squash at the lowest point (e.g. 0.05 = 5 % shorter, wider).\n- `lift?: number`: How high the lowest point of the swinging foot clears the floor at its peak (m).\n- `liftPeak?: number`: Where in the swing (0..1) the foot is highest (default 0.4).\n- `reach?: number`: Shift the whole stride forward (m).\n- `swingReach?: number`: Extra forward throw of the swinging foot late in the swing, before it settles (m).\n- `swingDelay?: number`: Swing timing: 0 = even, + = the foot hangs back then whips forward (running).\n- `plant?: number`: 0..1: how much the swinging foot sweeps back to meet the ground at touchdown. At 1 it arrives moving backwards at exactly the ground speed (still, in the world), so the foot plants without sliding; at 0 it eases to a stop relative to the hips and slides forwa…\n- `heelStrike?: number`: Toes-up angle at heel strike (deg).\n- `toeOff?: number`: Heel-raise angle at toe-off (deg).\n- `roll?: [heel: number, toe: number]`: Fraction of stance spent rolling off the heel / onto the toes.\n- `tiptoe?: number`: Walk on the balls of the feet: extra pitch through stance (deg), pivoting on the ball.\n- `swingPitch?: number`: Extra toes-down droop mid-swing (deg).\n- `dangle?: number`: How much the swinging foot hangs from the shin mid-swing (0..1, default 0.8).\n- `lean?: number`: Torso lean forward (deg) and twist of the shoulders against the legs (deg).\n- `twist?: number`\n- `sway?: number`: Side-to-side torso roll (deg).\n- `pelvisPitch?: number`: Pelvis pitch (deg): tips the whole body, legs are re-solved.\n- `head?: number`: Head pitch (deg) and how much it counters the torso twist (0..1).\n- `headSteady?: number`\n- `arms?: { /** Swing amplitude (deg). */ swing: number; /** Base elbow bend (deg). */ elbow: number; /** Extra elbow bend when the arm is forward (deg). */ pump?: numbe…`\n- `pose?: Pose`: Extra joints/overrides mixed into every frame (hands, hat...).\n- `extra?: (u: number, pose: Record<string, Euler | JointPose>) => void`: Per-frame hook for anything else: receives the cycle phase 0..1.\n- `step?: number`: Key spacing in frames (default 0.5 — IK is non-linear, keep it fine).\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### type Key\n\n```ts\ntype Key = readonly [number, Pose, Ease?]\n```\n\n`[frame, pose, easeToNextKey?]`\n\n### interface LegDef\n\n- `rest: readonly [number, number]`: Where the foot rests, in the body's space (x right, z forward; y ignored).\n- `partners?: readonly number[]`: Legs that must not be lifting when this one starts a step (its neighbours; mutual, so listing a pair once is enough).\n\n### class LegStepper\n\nFeet for a body with any number of legs. `update(dt, body, yaw, velocity, ground)`:\nthe body's world position and heading, how fast it moves, and the ground height at a point.\n\n- `new LegStepper(o: LegStepperOptions)`\n- `readonly feet: Foot[]`\n- `o: Required<Omit<LegStepperOptions, 'legs'>> & { legs: readonly LegDef[] }`\n- `steps: number`: Steps started (tests).\n- `target(i: number, body: Vec3, yaw: number, velocity: Vec3, ground: (x: number, z: number) => number, out: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0]): Vec3`: Where leg `i` wants to be: its rest spot around the body, ahead by the lead, on the ground.\n- `reset(body: Vec3, yaw: number, ground: (x: number, z: number) => number): void`: Every foot planted on its rest spot around `body` (a spawn or a teleport). The first `update` does this itself.\n- `update(dt: number, body: Vec3, yaw: number, velocity: Vec3, ground: (x: number, z: number) => number): void`\n- `planted(): number`: Feet on the ground now.\n\n### interface LegStepperOptions\n\n- `legs: readonly LegDef[]`\n- `threshold?: number`: Step when the foot is this far from its target (m). Default 0.3.\n- `stepTime?: number`: Seconds a step takes. Default 0.18.\n- `lift?: number`: How high a step lifts the foot (m). Default 0.15.\n- `lead?: number`: Aim ahead of the body's motion by this many seconds (feet land where it is going). Default 0.25.\n\n### mirror()\n\n```ts\nfunction mirror(pose: Pose, rig: Pick<RigSpec, 'mirror'>): Pose\n```\n\nMirror a pose left ↔ right (rig.mirror pairs; Y/Z rotations and X offsets flip).\n\n### placeFeet()\n\n```ts\nfunction placeFeet(pose: Pose, rig: RigSpec, goals: Partial<Record<Side, FootGoal>>): Pose\n```\n\nReturn `pose` with its legs solved so the feet land on the goals. Existing Y/Z\nrotations on the leg joints (spread, twist) are kept; X is replaced.\n\n### type Pose\n\n```ts\ntype Pose = Readonly<Record<string, Euler | JointPose>>\n```\n\nJoint name → rotation (degrees) or full joint pose. Missing joints are at rest.\n\n### renderCurves()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderCurves(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip, options: CurveOptions = {}): SheetImage\n```\n\n### renderSheet()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSheet(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip, options: SheetOptions = {}): SheetImage\n```\n\nRender a contact sheet for one clip.\n\n### interface RigSpec\n\nEverything the tools need to know about a rig beyond its hierarchy.\n\n- `joints: readonly string[]`: Joint names in hierarchy order (root first).\n- `root: string`\n- `mirror: Readonly<Record<string, string>>`: Left/right counterparts for mirroring.\n- `soles: readonly string[]`: Mesh names that touch the ground (soles).\n- `trace: readonly string[]`: Points to trace in contact sheets (joint or mesh names).\n- `limits: Readonly<Record<string, JointLimit>>`: Human-plausible rotation ranges, degrees.\n- `fps: number`: Frame rate of the authoring format.\n- `legs?: LegRig`: Leg geometry, enabling foot IK and the gait generator.\n- `spine?: { torso?: string; head?: string; cap?: string }`: Joints the runtime procedural layers turn (PoseLayers): the torso and head look where the character is going; `cap` is an optional springy joint (not animated by clips).\n\n### sampleClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction sampleClip(clip: ClipDef, frame: number, rig: RigSpec | readonly string[]): Record<string, ResolvedJoint>\n```\n\nEvaluate a clip's pose at `frame` (keys + layers + feet track), fully resolved for every\njoint of `rig` (or the listed joints — then the feet track needs a rig and is skipped).\n\n### interface SheetImage\n\nTiny RGBA software canvas shared by contact sheets, curve plots and filmstrips (no GPU, no DOM).\n\n- `width: number`\n- `height: number`\n- `data: Uint8ClampedArray`\n\n### class SpringChain\n\nA chain of points hanging off a moving root. Call `update(dt, root, basis)` every frame\nwith the root's world position and its orientation (three columns: right, up, forward),\nthen read `points` (root first).\n\n- `new SpringChain(o: SpringChainOptions)`\n- `readonly points: Vec3[]`\n- `o: Required<SpringChainOptions>`\n- `reset(root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`: Put the chain straight out in its rest pose (no swing).\n- `update(dt: number, root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`\n- `swing(basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): number`: How far the tip is from where the rest pose puts it (m): how much it is swinging.\n\n### interface SpringChainOptions\n\n- `segments: number`: Points after the root (≥ 1).\n- `length: number`: Length of each segment (m).\n- `rest?: Vec3`: Rest direction from the root in the root's own space (normalised; default straight back, −Z).\n- `stiffness?: number`: Pull toward the rest pose per second (0 floppy … 30 stiff). Default 10.\n- `keep?: number`: Velocity kept per 1/60 s (0..1). Default 0.94.\n- `gravity?: number`: Gravity on the chain (m/s²). Default 6 (lighter than real: tails hold up a bit).\n\n### class Squash\n\nSquash and stretch as a damped spring on `value` (0 = rest, + stretched, − squashed).\n`scale()` turns it into a volume-preserving pair: `const [h, v] = squash.scale();\nmesh.scale.set(h, v, h)`.\n\n- `value: number`\n- `velocity: number`\n- `stiffness: number`: Spring stiffness and damping (a critically damped spring has damping = 2√stiffness).\n- `damping: number`\n- `amount: number`: 0 turns it off; 2.5 is rubbery.\n- `kick(v: number): void`: Add a kick: negative squashes (a landing), positive stretches (a jump).\n- `set(v: number): void`: Set the shape directly (a held squash).\n- `update(dt: number): void`\n- `scale(out: [number, number] = [1, 1]): [number, number]`: [horizontal, vertical] scale: y = 1 + s, xz = 1 / √(1 + s), so the volume stays put.\n\n### twoBoneIK()\n\n```ts\nfunction twoBoneIK(root: Vec3, target: Vec3, a: number, b: number, pole: Vec3, out: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0]): Vec3\n```\n\nWhere the middle joint of a two-bone limb goes (a knee, an elbow) so the end reaches\n`target` from `root` with bones `a` and `b` long, bending toward `pole` (a point the knee\npoints at). Analytic (the law of cosines), no iterations; a target out of reach straightens\nthe limb toward it, one too close folds it as far as it goes. Returns the joint, into `out`.\n\n### validateClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction validateClip(clip: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec): string[]\n```\n\nProblems with a clip definition itself (unknown joints, bad key order, loop mismatch).\n\n## 13. Hero kit\n\n### HERO_CLIPS\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_CLIPS: readonly ClipDef[]\n```\n\n### HERO_MODEL\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_MODEL: \"assets/hero.glb\"\n```\n\n### HERO_RAGDOLL\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_RAGDOLL: readonly RagdollPart[]\n```\n\nThe hero as a ragdoll (the joints of `hero.glb`, see rig.ts): pelvis, torso and head, upper\narms and legs on ball joints held in cones, elbows and knees as hinges bending the way the\nclips bend them (Forearm −X, Shin +X). Hands and feet ride on their forearm and shin.\n\n### HERO_RIG\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_RIG: RigSpec\n```\n\nThe hero rig (public/assets/hero.glb, built by scripts/assets/hero.mjs).\n\nThe character faces +Z. L/R are the character's own sides (its right = -X).\nRotations are Euler XYZ degrees relative to rest; Z is applied first, then Y, then X,\nall about the parent's axes. Verified against contact sheets (`npm run anim -- sheet`).\n\n  Pelvis   +X tip forward (90 = face down, -90 = on its back, 360 = front flip)\n           +Y turn to its left, +Z roll to its RIGHT. p = [x, y, z] offset (m), s = squash\n  Torso    +X bend forward, +Y twist left, +Z lean right\n  Head     +X look down, +Y look left, +Z tilt right\n  Arm*     X swings: -90 = straight ahead, -180 = overhead, +40 = back\n           Z spreads (…\n\n## 14. three.js and Rapier\n\n### RAPIER\n","numLines":360,"startLine":3539,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KZapUKjPV5TyfkceWPG7TW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
